problem:
Problem description
- There is a set of products $$\mathcal{P}$$ indexed by $$p \in \mathcal{P}$$, a set of machines $$\mathcal{M}$$ indexed by $$m \in \mathcal{M}$$, and a planning horizon of periods $$\mathcal{T}$$ indexed by $$t \in \mathcal{T}$$.
- Each product $$p$$ has a processing time requirement on each machine $$m$$, represented by $$a_{m,p}$$, measured in a consistent time unit per standardized lot.
- Each machine $$m$$ has an available capacity in each period $$t$$, represented by $$K_{m,t}$$.
- Each product $$p$$ has an initial on-hand inventory $$I^{0}_{p}$$ at the start of the planning horizon.
- Each product $$p$$ has a forecast demand in each period $$t$$, represented by $$D_{p,t}$$.
- Production is allowed in fractional lots, so the production decision is continuous rather than integer.
- The planning objective is to maximize the total ending inventory across all products at the end of the planning horizon.
- All demand must be satisfied from available initial inventory plus production, and inventory may not become negative at any point in the planning horizon.
- Machine time usage created by production must not exceed the available capacity of each machine in each period.
- Inventory is tracked separately for each product across periods, with beginning inventory carried forward from one period to the next after production and demand fulfillment.
- No explicit cost or penalty terms are included in the objective for this instance.
- A solution is represented by:
  - The production quantity $$x_{p,t}$$, giving the number of standardized lots of product $$p$$ produced in period $$t$$.
  - The ending inventory level $$I_{p,t}$$, giving the inventory of product $$p$$ remaining at the end of period $$t$$.
  - The final objective value $$Z$$, giving the total ending inventory at the end of the horizon.

Input Format
{
  "P": "number of products $$|\mathcal{P}|$$",
  "M": "number of machines $$|\mathcal{M}|$$",
  "T": "number of planning periods $$|\mathcal{T}|$$",
  "products": [
    "product identifiers for each $$p \in \mathcal{P}$$"
  ],
  "machines": [
    "machine identifiers for each $$m \in \mathcal{M}$$"
  ],
  "periods": [
    "period identifiers for each $$t \in \mathcal{T}$$"
  ],
  "processing_time": {
    "shape": "$$M \\times P$$",
    "meaning": "Matrix of processing times $$a_{m,p}$$, where each entry is the time required on machine $$m$$ to produce one standardized lot of product $$p$$"
  },
  "machine_capacity": {
    "shape": "$$M \\times T$$",
    "meaning": "Matrix of available machine capacities $$K_{m,t}$$ for each machine $$m$$ in each period $$t$$"
  },
  "initial_inventory": {
    "shape": "$$P$$",
    "meaning": "Vector of starting inventories $$I^{0}_{p}$$ for each product $$p$$"
  },
  "demand": {
    "shape": "$$P \\times T$$",
    "meaning": "Demand matrix $$D_{p,t}$$ for each product $$p$$ in each period $$t$$"
  }
}

Output Format
{
  "production": {
    "shape": "$$P \\times T$$",
    "meaning": "Production plan $$x_{p,t}$$, giving the number of standardized lots of product $$p$$ produced in period $$t$$"
  },
  "ending_inventory": {
    "shape": "$$P \\times T$$",
    "meaning": "Inventory plan $$I_{p,t}$$, giving the ending inventory of product $$p$$ in period $$t$$"
  },
  "objective_value": {
    "shape": "$$1$$",
    "meaning": "Total ending inventory $$Z$$ at the end of the planning horizon"
  }
}

Example(s)

Input 1
{
  "P": 2,
  "M": 2,
  "T": 1,
  "products": ["liquid", "solid"],
  "machines": ["Machine 1", "Machine 2"],
  "periods": ["week 1"],
  "processing_time": [
    [50, 24],
    [30, 33]
  ],
  "machine_capacity": [
    [40],
    [35]
  ],
  "initial_inventory": [30, 90],
  "demand": [
    [75],
    [95]
  ]
}
